45417] [17592186134300 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c213b52-b119-57bd-9ee2-819532e6eca1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134300 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134300 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{v}$ and $\\text{Span}{w}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{2}{3}{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134671 #edb/ref 1061 20243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3723cbb7-65e2-58df-a3bc-b3e44a4dbf6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134672 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134672 #edb/ref 1061 20244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc352e2b-a661-5eae-80e6-33a272fb3132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134673 #edb/ref 1061 20245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea84ca60-824b-5c39-820b-45e1a3de351a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134767 #edb/ref 1061 26414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ffcde54-4ad3-5c96-bc53-f1313d194004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134768 #edb/ref 1061 26425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5dcbfead-f350-544f-a0c0-5ef1f197ee0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134769 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134769 #edb/ref 1061 26428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6328b011-7457-5a1a-b13f-1e2b37822525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134773 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134773 #edb/ref 1061 27054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134773 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42dfcb52-37d6-5cd0-9c79-bb0c59437b69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134773 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135048 #edb/ref 1064 "If $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}$ is a set of orthogonal vectors, then the **orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{n}}{{a}_{n}\\cdot {a}_{n}}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace of $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal!\n\nIn case of a $2$-dimensional subspace $S=\\textrm{Span} \\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\big\\},$ we can visualize the orthogonal projection geometrically, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x}} \n\t= \n\t{\\color{red}\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x}} + {\\color{red}\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}}\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d27747451504c7244ddac4609c9c205c60cd525de2cfd364c20e90c1655ef4e.png)\n\n**Note:** A proof of the formula will be provided at the end of the lesson. But first, let's get some practice using the formula." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135048 #edb/ref 1065 3625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135048 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "52ad8222-2949-5d1f-b34c-ea06e2a98ddc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135048 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135087 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now prove the formula for the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspaces $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1 \\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ given that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\perp \\mathbf{a}_2.$ \n\nWe start by writing $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S=\\text{proj}_S\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is in the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$. That is, $\\mathbf{x}_S=k_1\\mathbf{a}_1+k_2\\mathbf{a}_2\\in S\\,$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp.$\n\nFrom the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is orthogonal to $S,$ and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a}_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {a}_{1}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{1})=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {a}_{2}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{2})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})=x\\cdot {a}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=x\\cdot {a}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}} \\\\ {k}_{2}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain our formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135087 #edb/ref 1065 4345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135087 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "adabdbc0-f726-5b67-81e5-73ef172ea7cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135087 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification of the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135088 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135088 #edb/ref 1065 4346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135088 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0c1861f6-1be0-5e7e-88be-6186634715ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135088 #edb/ref 1067 "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269512 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1d9a3cfb-52c0-5be4-87c1-f76f5ae7b31a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270127 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270127 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270127 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61497d65-2033-5bdc-986b-b42a650cecc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270521 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f8461ba-1dae-5b54-80f1-6dbb83774e44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270526 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270526 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270526 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90b95c34-7216-5cb0-bccd-fe697695e561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271417 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5128844-b902-598e-8586-b0484fd37e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271849 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bb0c30a1-420f-58af-8caa-9fbfa56e943e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9852acca-5b7f-59fd-8378-ef1bf83dc23c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272067 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324202 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b6d61a2-0e5b-5db8-8114-ea09bd548880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324295 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2876536a-c600-53ac-b3c3-d18398b95b40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324430 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324430 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3895d9a2-5b55-56b8-8e77-b75947216